# Speed limits, in seconds, read by bin/jg-test-run.sh. `bin/jg-test-run.sh
# --show-limits` prints the effective values.
#
# A test that runs over its limit is REPORTED, never failed and never killed: the
# run prints the test's name, the time it measured and the limit. The class comes
# from the test's own name: anything ending -e2e.test.sh is an end-to-end test,
# everything else is a unit or policy test. A limit is a signal to look at a slow
# test, not a bound on a loaded runner (a real-Herdr script that ran 60.04 s was
# killed at 60 s and hid what it still had to prove).
#
# What bounds a hung test is a separate thing: the --changed ceiling, a caller's
# --per-script-timeout-secs, and the pull-request gate's whole-run budget,
# gate_wall_seconds, which bin/jg-local-suite.sh passes with --gate and which
# stops the run before the next test starts.
e2e_seconds=60
# unit_seconds is the FLOOR of a unit test's bound. A test's own bound is
# max(unit_seconds, 2 x its measured CI hint in bin/jg-test-run.sh); a test with
# no hint gets the floor, so a new slow test is still caught.
# Measured 2026-11-01: 5s was never a real limit. The old kill bound made every
# test slower than 5s read as ~5.03s; 39 of 100 hinted unit
# tests run over 5s and 25 over 15s (slowest jg-supervision-host 1162s,
# jg-watcher-lock 1060s). Do not tighten the floor below the quick tests'
# loaded runtime (~10s) without re-measuring.
unit_seconds=15
gate_wall_seconds=600
